    "body": "EICMA 2026 • MTPeak Stole Some of the Spotlight at EICMA 2026 With Insane Power and Retro Looks on Three Wheels\nFord Is Recalling F-150 Trucks in China for Improperly Secured Fuel Tank Straps\n6 photos\nPhoto: Ford\nFord has determined that 54 exported trucks may have been produced with fuel tanks that may detach from the F-150 without warning. Assembled in the United States of America, said half-ton pickups were shipped to China, where American trucks carry a very different cultural meaning from their utilitarian roots.\nPer a release from the Chinese State Administration for Market Regulation, the Ford Motor Company's local dealers will inspect F-150 vehicles produced for model years 2023 through 2025 from May 5, 2023, through October 16, 2024. Service technicians are to check both the front and rear fuel tank straps, where they attach to the frame's longitudinal beams.\nIf the straps appear to be iffy, dealers will replace them at no charge whatsoever to affected customers. Obviously enough, the potential consequences of an improperly secured fuel tank are pretty bad. The straps may loosen as the vehicle accumulates mileage, especially when it encounters the usual road imperfections.\nWhen the straps loosen too much, fuel might escape the tank, resulting in an engine stall. Dropping from the vehicle while driving is another possibility, increasing the risk of a fire while creating a hazard for traffic behind the subject pickup. The Dearborn-based automaker has already dealt with this problem in the United States as part of recall 26V578.\nInternally referred to as 26S69, the American campaign went live in September 2026. Similar to the Chinese recall, dealers were instructed to inspect the straps and replace them as they see fit. Both campaigns involve the very same F-150 generation, yet the Chinese recall is listed with fewer model years.\nPhoto: Ford China's F-150 issue further brings back memories of a single-vehicle recall from 2022, when a 2021 model was produced with the front fuel tank attachment bracket missing. At the time, Ford found no evidence that anyone at the plant had corrected the issue before giving the subject truck the green stamp of approval.\nThe current generation of the F-150 arrived in China in June 2022, first in the form of the Raptor off-road grade. Ford subsequently stopped sending US-built vehicles to China in April 2025, citing tariff escalation. China increased retaliatory tariffs at the time, with additional duties reaching a ginormous 125 percent.\nChina has since reduced the tariffs in question, and Ford's Chinese website continues promoting the F-150 Raptor. Care to guess how much this bad boy costs over there? 828,800 yuan is the listed starting price, converting to a staggering 124,350 dollars at current exchange rates. By comparison, the US-market Raptor is available from $75,325 (sans destination charge) at press time.\nBack home in the United States, the F-150 underwent a few changes for model year 2027, starting with a new 3.0-liter EcoBoost twin-turbo V6 as the replacement for the previous 2.7-liter unit. The 5.0-liter Coyote naturally aspirated V8, meanwhile, returned as an option on the King Ranch and Platinum.\nHands-free driving while towing also needs highlighting, along with lower-priced equipment groups for the Tremor and Raptor. Last but not least, the first-ever Ford F-150 Carhartt made its debut as a $4,195 package on the 4x4 XLT SuperCrew.\nVia // SAMR\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nFord F-150 Ford China recall safety\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Owner Spent $30,000 To Restore This Chevy Bel Air, Now Selling for $33,500",
